<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 wave 7, and doc 39's editor surface: the knobs, the look, what the node expands to,
    the resolved quality waterfall, the resolved volume fold, and Explode. The ledger sized it M in
    the seven-panel block with "port the readouts, keep the field rows (shape 2)" — and this panel is
    the one that instruction fits without qualification, because its field rows are not rows at all:
    they are two whole `InspectorView`s, which is shape 1 and always was.

    ⚠ **Two `InspectorView`s behind two `ref`s, and neither needs the wrapper `MaterialView` could
    not have.** `Inspect` is a method, `InspectorView` is `sealed`, and that combination is wave 6's
    sixth shape — but only *inside a `@for`*, where the call has to happen once per row and a
    property is the only thing a component tag can assign. One control fed once from `Restate` is
    ordinary shape 1: hold it in a member and call the method. The distinction is worth keeping
    because the two look identical from a distance and only one of them is blocked.

    ⚠ **`applying` survives the port unchanged, and it is the reason this panel is not simply four
    signals.** A write raises the document's `Changed`, which restates the panel, which re-`Inspect`s
    both forms — throwing away the row being typed into and the caret with it. The derived sections
    still rebuild; the guard covers exactly the two inspectors, and it has to stay a field rather
    than becoming a signal because nothing reads it from a binding.

    ⚠ **`analysis-list` is `AnalysisRow`'s fifth and sixth callers**, which is what wave 6 built the
    part for. Both lists here — what the node expands to, and what the volume fold said about itself
    — are `Line(into, stage, message, kind)`, which is the part's three elements exactly, with the
    severity passed as `class`. `AnalysisRow`'s host tag *is* the `analysis-row`, so the swap is
    byte-neutral.

    ⚠ **The quality table interleaves two different tags and the flag is in the key.** A
    `frame-group` heading and a `fact-row` are one list in the C# and have to be one `@for` here, so
    the choice is an `@if` inside the loop body — wave 4's trap — and `FrameKnobRow.Group` is in the
    key for `QueryView`'s reason. Unlike a selection, a row does not turn into a heading without
    everything else about it changing, so this costs nothing.

    ⚠ **`fact-value` is written with a caption here and with an interpolation in `FontView`, and both
    are right.** This panel does `row.Add("fact-value").Text = …` — the cell's *own* text, the
    ledger's shape 5, so `<FactValue Text="…" />`. `FontView` does
    `row.Add("fact-value").Add("text").Text = …`, which is what an interpolation builds. The ledger
    counted six hand-written fact rows and found the split was four to two; reconciling them is a
    layout change and not a tidy-up, so each port reproduces the one it replaces.

    ⚠ **`QualityRows` and `StackRows` stay synchronous, and the ledger's shape 3 is why the question
    was asked.** They are counts of the rows a projection produced, assigned while it runs — a signal
    *write* is immediate and only the tree is deferred — so a caller reading one on the line after
    `Restate` gets the same number it always did. ⚠ **Nothing in the tree is that caller.** Their
    remarks say "which is what a test counts" and no test counts them; grep finds no reference to
    `StandardFrameView` outside this file at all. Recorded rather than removed, because a public
    number this panel promises is not a wave-7 decision to revoke.

    ⚠ **The empty-table sentence asks `QualityKnobs.Length == 0` and not `QualityRows == 0`, and the
    difference is the whole of whether it works.** `QualityRows` is a plain `int` — a public counter
    this panel promises — so a `@if` reading it registers **no signal dependency at all**: the arm
    would be chosen once, at compose time, and never re-evaluated. It would have been chosen wrongly
    too, because `Show` runs before the first flush, so the table is already full when the arm is
    first picked and the sentence would never appear again however empty the table got.
    `QualityKnobs` reads the signal, which is what makes the arm re-run. The two are always equal —
    a group heading is only ever added immediately before a row — so nothing else changes.

    ⚠ **This is the general rule and it is easy to break by accident: a binding may only read a
    signal.** A field the panel also writes looks identical at the call site and is invisible to the
    compiler, and a whole-tree dump will not catch it either unless a dumped state happens to cross
    the branch — this one crossed it in none of six. `FrameViewTests` asserts the empty table
    directly, for that reason.

    ⚠ **The scroll region's shape is load-bearing and was measured on device.** `frame-knobs` is not a
    `ScrollView` of its own: a nested scroll region's content is `align-self: flex-start` with
    `min-width: 100%`, so its rows size against their own content's width and the fixed-width
    provenance column — the entire point of these tables — resolved to nothing and drew nothing. The
    panel scrolls as one thing. `<ScrollView>` takes its children through `ContentHost`, so the
    markup nests exactly where `scroll.Content.Add("frame-sections")` did.
-->
@component StandardFrameView
@namespace Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors.Frame
@inherits Vixen.Ui.Controls.Control
@tag frame-editor
@using System.Collections.Immutable
@using System.Globalization
@using Vixen.Core.Mathematics
@using Vixen.Ecs
@using Vixen.Editor.Core
@using Vixen.Editor.Inspector
@using Vixen.Editor.SceneView
@using Vixen.Rendering
@using Vixen.Rendering.Compositor
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive

<frame-banner ref="@Banner">@BannerText</frame-banner>

<ScrollView>
    <frame-sections>
        <WorldTitle Text="The frame" />

        <InspectorView ref="@Knobs" />

        <WorldTitle Text="Look profile" />

        <InspectorView ref="@Look" />

        <verb-row>
            <Button ref="@Explode"
                    Label="Explode to a full document"
                    Disabled="@CannotExplode"
                    on:click="@(() => Eject())" />
        </verb-row>

        <WorldTitle Text="What it expands to" />

        <analysis-list ref="@Facts">
            @for (var note in FactRows) {
                <AnalysisRow key="@note" class="@note.Class" Stage="@note.Stage" Message="@note.Message" />
            }
        </analysis-list>

        <WorldTitle Text="Resolved quality" />

        <CheckBox Label="Only what is overridden" change:IsChecked="@(only => Only(only))" />

        <frame-knobs ref="@Quality">
            @for (var knob in QualityKnobs) {
                @if (knob.Group) {
                    <FrameGroup key="@knob" Text="@knob.Name" />
                } else {
                    <fact-row key="@knob" class="@knob.Class">
                        <FactName Text="@knob.Name" />
                        <FactValue Text="@knob.Value" />
                    </fact-row>
                }
            }

            @if (QualityKnobs.Length == 0) {
                <TextLine Text="Nothing above the engine table states a value for this tier." />
            }
        </frame-knobs>

        <WorldTitle Text="Volumes reaching the camera" />

        <Button Label="Fold again" on:click="@(() => RestateVolumes())" />

        <analysis-list ref="@Summary">
            @if (NoScene.Length > 0) {
                <TextLine Text="@NoScene" />
            }

            @for (var note in SummaryRows) {
                <AnalysisRow key="@note" class="@note.Class" Stage="@note.Stage" Message="@note.Message" />
            }
        </analysis-list>

        <frame-knobs ref="@Stack">
            @for (var knob in StackKnobs) {
                <fact-row key="@knob" class="@knob.Class">
                    <FactName Text="@knob.Name" />
                    <FactValue Text="@knob.Value" />
                </fact-row>
            }
        </frame-knobs>
    </frame-sections>
</ScrollView>

@code {
    readonly ResolvedVolumes volumes = new();

    /// <summary>What the panel is saying about the document as a whole.</summary>
    readonly Signal<string> banner = new(string.Empty);

    /// <summary>Whether the node can be replaced by the graph it stands for.</summary>
    readonly Signal<bool> explodable = new(false);

    /// <summary>What the expansion produced, and what it complained about.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<FrameNote>> facts = new([]);

    /// <summary>What the fold said about itself.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<FrameNote>> summary = new([]);

    /// <summary>The sentence shown instead of a fold when no scene is open.</summary>
    readonly Signal<string> noScene = new(string.Empty);

    /// <summary>The resolved quality stack, headings and rows in one list.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<FrameKnobRow>> quality = new([]);

    /// <summary>The resolved volume stack.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<FrameKnobRow>> stack = new([]);

    StandardFrameDocument? document;
    bool overriddenOnly;

    /// <summary>Whether the panel is the thing that caused the change it is being told about.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Without it, editing a knob rebuilds the form the knob is in.</b> A write raises the
    ///     document's <c>Changed</c>, and the full restate re-inspects both forms — which throws away
    ///     the row being typed into, and with it the caret. The derived sections still rebuild; only
    ///     the two inspectors are left alone.
    /// </remarks>
    bool applying;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool AcceptsFocus => false;

    /// <summary>Where custom inspectors are found, so the markup forms are used rather than the rows.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Handed in rather than read off <c>EditorRegistry.Default</c>.</b> The static is a
    ///     different session's in a test run — the reason <c>TerrainModulePanels</c> gives — and a
    ///     panel that found somebody else's contribution for its own settings type would draw
    ///     somebody else's form. Null is not a failure: the generated rows are drawn instead, in
    ///     declaration order, and everything still edits.
    /// </remarks>
    public IEditorRegistry? Extensions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The scene whose volumes the stack panel folds, or null for an editor without one.</summary>
    public IActiveScene? Scene { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The view the editor is looking through, whose position decides which volumes reach.</summary>
    public IActiveView? Eye { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The knobs.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A <c>ref</c>, and ordinary shape 1 rather than wave 6's <c>sealed</c> blocker.</b>
    ///     One control fed once by a method needs no wrapper type; what <c>sealed</c> stops is the
    ///     wrapper a control fed <i>inside a <c>@for</c></i> would need, which is
    ///     <c>MaterialView</c>'s parameter list and not this.
    /// </remarks>
    public InspectorView Knobs { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The look profile the document carries inline.</summary>
    /// <inheritdoc cref="Knobs" />
    public InspectorView Look { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The button that replaces the node with the graph it stands for.</summary>
    public Button Explode { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What the expansion produced, and what it complained about.</summary>
    public UiElement Facts { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The resolved quality stack.</summary>
    public UiElement Quality { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The resolved volume stack.</summary>
    public UiElement Stack { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What the fold said about itself — the camera, the counts, and what did not reach.</summary>
    public UiElement Summary { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What the panel is saying about the document as a whole.</summary>
    public UiElement Banner { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>How many quality rows are showing, which is what a test counts.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Still synchronous, and the ledger's shape 3 is why the question was asked.</b> It is
    ///     assigned while the projection runs, and a signal write is immediate — only the tree it
    ///     produces is deferred. ⚠ And no test counts it: nothing in the tree names
    ///     <c>StandardFrameView</c> outside this file. Kept because a public number this panel
    ///     promises is not a port's to revoke.
    /// </remarks>
    public int QualityRows { get; private set; }

    /// <summary>And how many volume-parameter rows.</summary>
    /// <inheritdoc cref="QualityRows" />
    public int StackRows { get; private set; }

    partial void OnComposed() {
        Knobs.EditedDocument = null;
        Look.EditedDocument = null;
    }

    /// <summary>Shows a frame document.</summary>
    /// <param name="frame">The document.</param>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException"><paramref name="frame" /> is null.</exception>
    public void Show(StandardFrameDocument frame) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(frame);

        if (document is { } previous) {
            previous.Changed -= Restate;
        }

        document = frame;

        Knobs.Extensions = Extensions ?? Knobs.Extensions;
        Look.Extensions = Extensions ?? Look.Extensions;

        frame.Changed += Restate;

        Restate(frame);
    }

    /// <summary>Stops listening to the document, because a view that has left must not write.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b><c>OnUnmounted</c> rather than <c>OnRemoved</c>, and the generator owns the
    ///     difference.</b> A markup component's <c>OnRemoved</c> is emitted — it is where the
    ///     composition is disposed — so overriding it here is <c>CS0111</c>. <c>OnUnmounted</c> is
    ///     the partial the generator calls first, before the effects go, which is exactly where a
    ///     subscription to something outside the document belongs.
    /// </remarks>
    partial void OnUnmounted() {
        if (document is { } frame) {
            frame.Changed -= Restate;
            document = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Rebuilds everything the document decides.</summary>
    /// <param name="frame">The document.</param>
    public void Restate(StandardFrameDocument frame) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(frame);

        document = frame;

        // ⚠ The banner is the transition doc 39 asks for. After an explode the file is a full
        // document and the form over it is gone; a panel that kept drawing knobs would be a form
        // whose every write was discarded, which is the silent half of "one-way".
        banner.Value = frame.CanEdit
            ? $"!StandardFrame — {(frame.TierIsHosts ? "no tier written, so the host decides" : $"tier {frame.Tier}")}"
            : "A hand-authored document. There is no frame node to turn, so the knobs are hidden and "
            + "the panel does not write this file.";

        if (!applying) {
            Knobs.Inspect(frame.CanEdit ? [frame.Settings] : []);
            Look.Inspect(frame.CanEdit ? [frame.Look] : []);

            Watch(Knobs);
            Watch(Look);
        }

        explodable.Value = frame.CanExplode;

        RestateFacts(frame);
        RestateQuality();
        RestateVolumes();
    }

    /// <summary>Subscribes to every row of a form, so a write reaches the document.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b><c>InspectorView.ValueChanged</c> does not fire for a custom inspector, which is the
    ///     shape this panel is.</b> The event is raised from the generated-row loop, and a
    ///     <c>CustomInspector</c> returns before that loop runs — so a markup form's writes are
    ///     silent to whoever is holding the view. The edit target's own properties do raise
    ///     <c>Changed</c>, and they are the same objects the markup bound to, so this is the same
    ///     notification one layer down rather than a second mechanism.
    /// </remarks>
    void Watch(InspectorView view) {
        if (view.Edited is not { } edited) {
            return;
        }

        // ⚠ A fresh `InspectorTarget` is built by every `Inspect`, so these are new properties with
        // no subscribers rather than the same ones subscribed twice.
        foreach (var property in edited.Properties()) {
            property.Changed += _ => Applied();
        }
    }

    void Applied() {
        if (applying || document is not { } frame) {
            return;
        }

        applying = true;

        try {
            // The document raises `Changed`, which is what restates the panel — so the write path
            // and the reload path are the same path and cannot drift.
            frame.Apply();
        } finally {
            applying = false;
        }
    }

    void Only(bool value) {
        overriddenOnly = value;

        RestateQuality();
    }

    void Eject() {
        if (document is not { } frame || !frame.CanExplode) {
            return;
        }

        try {
            var kept = frame.Explode();

            facts.Value = [
                new(
                    0,
                    string.Empty,
                    "exploded",
                    $"The knobs are gone and this file is now hand-authored. What was there is beside it, "
                    + $"as {Path.GetFileName(kept)}."
                )
            ];
        } catch (InvalidOperationException refusal) {
            facts.Value = [new(0, "error", "refused", refusal.Message)];
        }
    }

    void RestateFacts(StandardFrameDocument frame) {
        var expanded = frame.Expanded;
        var rows = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<FrameNote>();
        var slot = 0;

        // Said on success too, for the reason the compositor's own analysis gives: a list that
        // empties itself when everything is fine is one nobody can tell apart from one that never ran.
        rows.Add(
            new(
                slot++,
                string.Empty,
                "frame",
                string.Create(
                    CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                    $"{expanded.Stages.Length} stages, {expanded.Resources.Length} targets, "
                    + $"{expanded.Buffers.Length} buffers."
                )
            )
        );

        rows.Add(
            new(
                slot++,
                string.Empty,
                "quality",
                frame.Preset is null
                    ? $"Engine defaults for {frame.Tier}. No {StandardFrameDocument.PresetFile} beside the frame."
                    : $"{StandardFrameDocument.PresetFile} over the engine table, for {frame.Tier}."
            )
        );

        foreach (var complaint in frame.Diagnostics) {
            rows.Add(new(slot++, "error", "refused", complaint));
        }

        facts.Value = rows.ToImmutable();
    }

    void RestateQuality() {
        QualityRows = 0;

        if (document is not { } frame) {
            quality.Value = [];
            return;
        }

        var rows = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<FrameKnobRow>();
        var group = string.Empty;
        var slot = 0;

        foreach (var knob in ResolvedQualityTable.Resolve(frame.Tier, frame.Preset, frame.Node?.Preset)) {
            if (overriddenOnly && !knob.Overridden) {
                continue;
            }

            if (!string.Equals(group, knob.Group, StringComparison.Ordinal)) {
                group = knob.Group;

                rows.Add(new(slot++, true, string.Empty, group, string.Empty));
            }

            // ⚠ The provenance is *in* the value cell, not in a column of its own, and that is a
            // finding rather than a preference. A third cell with a fixed width sits at the right
            // edge of a docked panel, where a long member name, a padded scroll region or a
            // scrollbar each push it out of view — measured three ways on device, invisible every
            // time. A cell that always draws is worth more than a column that sometimes aligns.
            //
            // ⚠ Short names, because the cell is narrow: `RenderQuality.vxpreset` clipped to
            // `RenderQuality.vxpres…` says less than `vxpreset`. The file's full name is on the
            // line above the table, once.
            //
            // ⚠ Engine values are the rule and the other two are the exceptions, so only the
            // exceptions are coloured — a table where every row is highlighted highlights nothing.
            rows.Add(
                new(
                    slot++,
                    false,
                    knob.Overridden ? "overridden" : string.Empty,
                    knob.Name,
                    $"{knob.Value}   {knob.Layer switch {
                        QualityLayer.Document => "document",
                        QualityLayer.Project => "vxpreset",
                        _ => "engine"
                    }}"
                )
            );

            QualityRows++;
        }

        quality.Value = rows.ToImmutable();
    }

    void RestateVolumes() {
        StackRows = 0;

        if (document is not { } frame) {
            stack.Value = [];
            summary.Value = [];
            noScene.Value = string.Empty;

            return;
        }

        if (Scene?.Current is not { } scene) {
            stack.Value = [];
            summary.Value = [];
            noScene.Value = "No scene is open, so there is nothing to fold.";

            return;
        }

        noScene.Value = string.Empty;

        // ⚠ The document's own look is laid down as the base layer, because that is what the host
        // does: `PostEffectFactory` deposits it on the builder and `AppGraphics` hands it to the
        // fold. A panel that folded volumes without it would show a stack the game never has.
        volumes.Look = frame.Look.ToSettings();
        volumes.Camera = Eye?.Current?.Position ?? Vector3.Zero;

        var report = volumes.Fold(scene.World);
        var lines = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<FrameNote>();
        var line = 0;

        // ⚠ Above the table rather than in it, and not only so it stays put while the parameters
        // scroll: a sentence is as wide as it is, and a scroll region sized to its content is a
        // region whose columns start off the right edge — which is where the provenance column went
        // the first time these four lines shared the table's box.
        lines.Add(new(line++, string.Empty, "camera", Where(volumes.Camera)));
        lines.Add(new(line++, string.Empty, "fold", report.Summary));

        if (report.HasLook) {
            lines.Add(
                new(line++, string.Empty, "look", "The document's look profile is the base layer, at full weight.")
            );
        }

        if (report.Volumes > report.Contributing) {
            // ⚠ Two short lines rather than one long one, and the reason is the panel's width: a
            // sentence in this column wraps against the scroll content's width rather than the
            // dock's, so a line past about sixty characters runs off the right edge mid-word. The
            // cause list is the useful half and must not be the half that is cut.
            lines.Add(
                new(line++, "warning", "note", $"{report.Volumes - report.Contributing} placed and not reaching.")
            );

            lines.Add(
                new(line++, "warning", "why", "Out of range, zero weight, zero extents, or says nothing.")
            );
        }

        summary.Value = lines.ToImmutable();

        var rows = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<FrameKnobRow>();
        var slot = 0;

        foreach (var parameter in report.Parameters) {
            // The same three facts the quality table shows, in the same cell and for the same
            // reason: what it resolved to, at what weight, and which layer had the last word.
            //
            // ⚠ Contested is the interesting state, not "came from a volume". A parameter one
            // layer claims is doing what its author asked; a parameter three layers claim is
            // where somebody's edit is being quietly out-voted.
            rows.Add(
                new(
                    slot++,
                    false,
                    parameter.IsContested ? "overridden" : string.Empty,
                    parameter.Parameter,
                    string.Create(
                        CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                        $"{parameter.Value} ×{parameter.Weight:0.##}   {Short(parameter.Winner)}"
                        + $"{(parameter.IsContested ? $" of {parameter.Layers.Count}" : string.Empty)}"
                    )
                )
            );

            StackRows++;
        }

        stack.Value = rows.ToImmutable();
    }

    /// <summary>A layer name that fits the column.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Shortened here and not in <see cref="ResolvedVolumes" />.</b> The fold's own spelling
    ///     is <c>volume(priority 3)</c> — <c>PostProcessVolumeSystem.Contributions</c> writes it, a
    ///     log line quotes it, and a test asserts it — so the data keeps it and only the cell is
    ///     narrowed. A clipped <c>volume(prior…</c> tells a reader nothing; the priority is the part
    ///     that distinguishes one volume from the next.
    /// </remarks>
    static string Short(string layer) => layer.StartsWith("volume(priority ", StringComparison.Ordinal)
        ? $"priority {layer[16..].TrimEnd(')')}"
        : layer;

    static string Where(Vector3 point) => string.Create(
        CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
        $"{point.X:0.##}, {point.Y:0.##}, {point.Z:0.##}"
    );

    // ================================================================== What the markup reads

    string BannerText => banner.Value;

    bool CannotExplode => !explodable.Value;

    ImmutableArray<FrameNote> FactRows => facts.Value;

    ImmutableArray<FrameNote> SummaryRows => summary.Value;

    string NoScene => noScene.Value;

    ImmutableArray<FrameKnobRow> QualityKnobs => quality.Value;

    ImmutableArray<FrameKnobRow> StackKnobs => stack.Value;
}
